9141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1031 "Understanding Proportional Relationships From Descriptions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1033 2554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "de04f42d-8248-5b77-8683-487f694fba77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1031 "Modeling with Direct Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1033 2274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e9994744-cb83-57d9-8154-ea5ac4c05cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "458ebd28-0d5b-5e34-ac8f-0865001d94d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1071 "The force $F$, measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a$, measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$. When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$, the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$. What is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is\n\n$$\na=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2\n$$\n\nthe force is\n\n$$\nF=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2000 &= k \\cdot (5) \\\\\n2000 &= 5k \\\\\n\\frac{2000}{5} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{F}{a}\n$$\n\nwe have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{kg m/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} &= \\frac{kgm/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} \\\\\n&= kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nk = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1071 "The stretch $s$, measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$, of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l$, measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g})$. When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g}$, the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1074 "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l$, so we have\n\n$s = k{l}$,\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when\n\n$$\nl=8\\,\\mathrm{g}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\ns=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9.6 &= k \\cdot (8) \\\\\n9.6 &= 8k \\\\\n\\frac{9.6}{8} &= k \\\\\n1.2 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nl=6 \\,\\textrm{g}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1071 "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L$, measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m$, then what equation models this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n$F = kL^2$,\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nF=4\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nL=0.1\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= k \\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\\n4 &= k \\cdot 0.01 \\\\\n\\frac{4}{0.01} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n$F = 400L^2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Constant of Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 